            0.636,
            0.107
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 5.2: Let us represent solubility graphically"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.107,
            0.11,
            0.777,
            0.196
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Consider water as the solvent, and compounds ‘A’ and ‘B’ as the solutes. Each substance has a different solubility. A graph of solubility versus temperature is called a "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "solubility curve",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". The solubility curves for ‘A’ and ‘B’ as solutes are shown in Fig. 5.6. The x-axis shows temperature (°C), whereas the y-axis indicates the solubility of the solute in grams per 100 g of water."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.141,
            0.207,
            0.742,
            0.457
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 4,
              "bbox": [
                0.141,
                0.207,
                0.742,
                0.457
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_5_image_4.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 5,
              "bbox": [
                0.228,
                0.459,
                0.656,
                0.475
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 5.6: Solubility curves of compounds ‘A’ and ‘B’ in water"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.107,
            0.496,
            0.777,
            0.548
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Based on the information from the above graph, predict which of the two compounds, ‘A’ or ‘B’, will dissolve more in a given amount of water at a given temperature?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.55,
            0.725,
            0.569
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Observe Fig. 5.6 and fill in the blanks of the following statements:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.571,
            0.776,
            0.605
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(i) The solubility of compound ‘A’ in water at 20 °C is (less than/more than/similar to) its solubility at 60 °C."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.605,
            0.776,
            0.639
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(ii) The solubility of compound ‘B’ at 20 °C is (less than/ more than/similar to) its solubility at 60 °C."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.14,
            0.639,
            0.776,
            0.673
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(iii) The solubility of ______ increases more than that of ___ with an increase in the temperature."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.107,
            0.676,
            0.776,
            0.712
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What do you think will happen if you make a saturated solution at a higher temperature and cool it slowly? Let us find out!"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.107,
            0.724,
            0.775,
            0.746
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5.3 Methods of Separation of Homogeneous Mixtures"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.754,
            0.339,
            0.774
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5.3.1 Crystallization"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.106,
            0.778,
            0.778,
            0.914
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You have learnt to prepare a saturated solution. If you take a saturated solution of compound ‘B’ in water at "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "6 0 ~ ^ { \\circ } \\mathrm { C } ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " prepared by dissolving 287 g of compound ‘B’ in 100 g of water, what will happen if you gradually cool it to 40 °C? As per Fig. 5.6, the solubility of compound ‘B’ is 241 g per 100 g of water at "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "4 0 ~ ^ { \\circ } \\mathrm { C } ."
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " It implies that only 241 g of compound ‘B’ can remain in the solution and the rest of it will separate out as a pure solid, often as crystals. A "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "crystal",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is a solid that is made up of particles arranged in a regular geometric pattern."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.783,
            0.785,
